we start with some breaking news in the  last few minutes from the supreme court  and that is the long-awaited judgment  in the test case dispute over claims of  business interruption insurance due to  the pandemic  and the court has uh handed a win to the  financial conduct  authority and small businesses against  insurers the supreme court upheld  appeals  by the fca and dismissed appeals by the  insurers this landmark win for  small firms against six of the world's  largest insurance companies  well joining me now to talk about this  is mark killick he's from the steering  committee of the victorious claimants to  his cox action group  and richard leedham he is the head of  commercial litigation  at mishkan torreira the law firm which  has represented them in this case  gentlemen welcome to you both richard  let me start with you if i may please i  mean this is a  horrendously complex case here can you  just explain  what the supreme court has said yeah  actually in um it's not that complex um  what the supreme court has said is that  in insurance policies  that cover business interruption where  the loss  is not tied to property damage  then if a business has suffered  interruption because of the government  actions due to that business should be  covered for its losses  one of the main points of law here was  around the  the sort of vicinity in which uh a  business had suffered a lockdown or a  closure wasn't it  correct and and on that the the supreme  court has been clear to say  that there has to be an occurrence of  covered within vicinity but it goes on  to say that in terms of what has caused  the loss  that each of those um cases of coded  throughout the country  are all equally effective causes of the  government  mandated lockdown back in march and so  so long as there is one case of covid  within the relevant vicinity  there is cover well uh mark let me bring  you in uh you must be highly delighted  you got the champagne  cracking open yet it it's an  extraordinary outcome ian i mean  the supreme court upheld all the policy  holders  uh uh grounds for appeal they upheld  none  of the of of the insurers uh uh  appeal grounds it's great news for the  30 000 uh policy holders at his cox his  cox  have been utterly humiliated i think  they led the insurers  and they led the insurance in the wrong  direction the fca  say 370 000 uh policyholders  across the uk could benefit from this  and given how many businesses are  literally flattened their backs  desperate for this money finally for the  supreme court to come and be so clear  as to what should be done is is just a  great result i mean it is an absolute  landmark judgment  mark presumably though this is tinged  with sadness on the basis that a lot of  the companies that were waiting for uh  payouts from their insurers are no  longer around to uh  benefit from them and you're so right  i mean in our own group the the his cops  action group uh we have 400 members  uh at least five or six of them have  already gone under and many others are  actually  teetering on the brink the key thing  here ian  is that the insurers now start to pay  out and of course there's still a  concern  that even given the supreme court  judgment they won't do that when you  like to believe they're now going to  start  doing the right thing and paying out on  their policies but  i'm not entirely convinced that that's  going to happen why do you say that  well i don't know what you know you'd  like to think for the best but there are  you know you wonder whether the insurers  will actually embrace this where they  will say that this is clear and  unambiguous  or whether they start nickel and diming  on every single point  you know what what britain's businesses  want is that the policies that they  bought  are honored mark we've spoken to you on  a number of occasions about this case  just to remind  uh people who who may not be familiar  with your story what how you were  affected by all this  well you know i i i i work for a pr  company media media zoo  we were impacted as many many other  businesses were by uh  by kobe we checked our insurance it was  crystal clear  that it triggered we are we we are we  applied to have our  our losses mitigated by the insurers  like many many other businesses across  the uk  and we're astonished to find that his  scots has turned us down flat  his cox said in april that their  policies do not apply to pandemics they  said it in terms as a market statement  nine months later the supreme court is  saying that's  absolute nonsense what were they  thinking  richard marx just raised the possibility  of in his words the insurance companies  now nickel and diming  every single claim would small  businesses have legal recourse if the  insurers started to do that  um yes they do they've got one major  tool in their armoury now that perhaps  wasn't there before  in that whilst it's true that each claim  has to be adjusted it's  of course not right that an insured says  well i've lost  a hundred thousand pounds mr insurer  please pay up the claim has to go  through a quantum process  but that has been going on in the  background on a lot of the claims  in any event so that that's mark's point  is that that should not  now be held up and the court has also  clarified that  um any any sort of downturn in the  business  before the claim was triggered due to  covid should be ignored in any  calculation of that so you just look at  what the business was doing last year  what it's done this year  the calculation should be simple but but  the other tool in the armory  point that the insureds have and it's i  think  implicit in the in the judgment is that  this should never have happened the the  court has said it's really clear  what should have happened and the  insurer's position was that effectively  cover was illusory  and they made that point to the insureds  just at the time  when the insureds needed insurers to  step in  as a result of that we think there's a  good case for most insureds to have a  claim  for damages for late payment of of their  insurance money on top of  their insurance claim and that's  something that lots of lawyers are going  to be looking at  and lots of those claims are going to be  put forward as well as claims  for the actual money that's due under  the policy  that's a really interesting point i mean  how much could those claims run into do  you think  that is a that is a good question  because this  um this route to recourse which came in  under the enterprise act and is  enshrined in the insurance act has never  to our knowledge been tested in court  before and  the damage will be assessed probably on  an individual basis  but it seems to us this is a paradigm  case of where  that remedy should be available to  insurers and it's simply not good enough  for the insurance market to say well  we had to go through this test case the  way the law works  is is is a judgment sets out what the  law has always been  these claims should have been paid and  agreed to be paid back in march  and although the supreme court judgment  is  is is it is to be applauded and the way  the fca  ran the case is to be applauded um it's  it's um absolutely apparent that it was  the appeal of the first instance  decision was driven by insurers  and that has delayed things by another  four months the first instance decision  came out  on the 15th of september and this  judgment is largely  an affirmation in an even more  comprehensive way it's true  of that judgment so another four months  has effectively been wasted well hang on  i mean when the fca and the insurers  agree to take this through the legal  process there was  an agreement written into this whichever  side lost in the first outcome there  would be a fast track appeals process  i mean if the small businesses had lost  first time round they would have taken  this to the supreme court wouldn't they  well that the appeal was envisaged  you're right that's a good point but  it's still  um it could have been avoided is my  point and when you're looking at the  overall  um time you're back to mark's examples  of businesses  that have failed in the period between  september and january  that could have been avoided  so it's all it's also worth saying that  the judgment is clear i mean first of  all it says  that if the insurers were right  the insurance they were offering was  illusory which is pretty damning  and more than that it says that the  insurer's position was clearly contrary  to the spirit  and the intent of the relevant  provisions  that is getting precious close to saying  the insurers have  tried to cheat do you have any sympathy  for the insurers i mean to go back to  your point about them so-called nickel  and diming claims i mean  they are going to want to send in the  loss of justice aren't they i mean we've  seen  for example from the furlough scheme  there's been an awful lot of fraud  that's gone on around this pandemic it's  not unreasonable for the insurers is it  to want to check that  people have lost exactly what they're  claiming  i don't think it is unreasonable but i  do think it's unreasonable of the  insurers to have dragged this out for  nine months  to have forced us to form a group  which eventually dragged the fca into  this  uh to fight and fight to get what we  paid for  it used to be that the insurance  industry said my word is my bond  whatever happened to that they've  trashed the reputation  of britain's insurers right across the  piece now you know  is cox has been humiliated by by  by this by this decision and i think all  worth  saying is it's time to pay up guys it's  time to do the right thing  mark do you think uh companies like you  in future will take out this sort of  business interruption cover  well i certainly won't take it out with  his [ __ ] here and that's for certain  [Laughter]  but uh to richard's point about the way  the fca has handled this  do you think do you agree with him that  the fca has uh actually done the right  thing  by by consumers here and and the  regulated  in this occasion the sca have done an  amazing job  there's no doubt both in the way that  they stepped in after  after we alerted i think the country to  what was going on here  they've driven it through the hizcox  action group  and and richard leader in michigan have  been right there alongside them and we  were intervenors right up the supreme  court of course  so we were alongside them but there's no  question the sa have done a fantastic  job  uh for small businesses across the uk  today

